Pushed a partial implementation of the argument configuration tests.  Every good and bad combination of arguments is run through a test model and tested to have either the correct configuration or fail appropriately.  I still need to split up the tests into each base argument and then test the interactions between the method argument and all others, but that shouldn't be hard to finish tomorrow.  Incidentally, I found a few bugs (some serious, some minor) building the tests.

Also, the nesterov optimization is failing for some reason.  I'll investigate tomorrow, but if anyone has any ideas I am all ears.

On Fri, Jul 19, 2013 at 5:44 PM, Michael Betancourt <betan...@gmail.com> wrote:
> Okay, I just checked in my idea.  Give it a try by running pulling, running "mkfast test/gm/arguments/argument_configuration", and then checking the output in test/gm/arguments/*.config.
>
> The basic idea is that the argument_probe class runs through the argument tree.  For each valued argument (list and singleton) it varies possible inputs (all valid values plus a "fail" value for list arguments, and good and bad values for singleton arguments that must be defined in each implementation) and then prints out the resulting tree from the original argument (all other values are set to defaults).  Tests will be broken up by the top list of arguments (method, id, data, output, random, etc).
>
> The tests would then take these files, convert them to an argument string (basically by removing newlines and whitespace), and then try to run them through an actual model to validate success.  I just wanted to see if you guys agreed with the general strategy or not before proceeding.  Thoughts?
>
> Dan, do you know of a way to run an equivalent of `ls` from within the tests?
>
>
> On Fri, Jul 19, 2013 at 9:41 AM, Michael Betancourt <betan...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > Right now I'm only considering the testing of argument specification, i.e. are all valid inputs actually valid?  Testing valid values is another test altogether and one that is not heavily tested in the current version of Stan.  Again, I think this can be readily automated by leveraging the argument tree in argument parser, but it will take some time to build the framework.
> >
> > On Jul 19, 2013, at 1:23 AM, Bob Carpenter <ca...@alias-i.com> wrote:
> >
> > > But what if the parameters are continuous?  We have to use some judgment to
> > > test reasonable values, like negative ones if there's a positivity constraint.
> >
>

